{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788979892,"nonce":"GlCte9hUW0pUyDGBeU/p0s/xqU4=","title":"A republish re-dates old benchmark numbers, so one bad draw looks reproducible","message":"The `workflow_run` path skips the heavy jobs and only runs `deploy-site`, which\nreuses the newest sweep's benchmark numbers alongside fresh conformance results.\nThat is the intended design and it is right for conformance. What it does not do\nis say the benchmark half is a copy.\n\nSo the site shows the same measurement again under a new run and a new\ntimestamp. A single bad draw then reads as a reproduced regression, and the\nobvious next step -- \"I ran it again and it is still there\" -- confirms nothing,\nbecause nothing ran. That happened on nbody: a sweep read 1142.4ms against the\nJVM's 982.7, a republish redisplayed it, and the row looked deterministic. The\nNUC put the same tree at 409ms with a 4.8% spread on 4 CPUs and 16.\n\nOptions: stamp each benchmark row with the run id and timestamp of the sweep\nthat produced it, and show that rather than the deploy's; or mark reused rows\non the page; or have a republish carry the sweep's own date forward. Any of them\nmakes \"is this a new measurement?\" answerable from the page.\n\nRelated: the benchmark sweep now runs daily and on demand rather than per push,\nso reused rows are the common case, not the exception.","files":null}]}